/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Emotions and Relationships: grief, envy, forgiveness, shame, nostalgia, awe, apologies and siblings */
WB.L("grief-how-we-mourn", "emotions", "intermediate", "Grief: what really happens when we lose someone",
  "The famous five stages of grief were never meant to be a map. Here is what researchers have learned about how people actually carry loss, and why there is no single right way to do it.",
  [
    ["Not a staircase", "The five stages of grief began as observations of dying patients, not a rulebook for mourners.",
      "In 1969 the Swiss-American psychiatrist Elisabeth Kübler-Ross published On Death and Dying, describing denial, anger, bargaining, depression and acceptance. She had been listening to people who were terminally ill, not to people who had lost someone. Over time the stages escaped the book and became a checklist, and grieving people sometimes worried they were doing it wrong because they skipped anger or circled back to denial.\n\nLater research has not found that people move through fixed stages in a set order. Kübler-Ross herself later said the stages were never meant to tidy messy emotions into neat packages. They can still be useful words for feelings that turn up, but grief is less a staircase than weather: changeable, overlapping and different for everyone."],
    ["Grief lives in the body", "Grief is a whole-body response to loss, not weakness or self-indulgence.",
      "People who are grieving often describe it physically: a heavy chest, a hollow stomach, exhaustion that sleep does not fix. Many cannot concentrate, lose things or reread the same page again and again, a fog sometimes called grief brain. Appetite can vanish or swing the other way. These reactions make sense, because losing someone close is a major stress and the body's stress systems respond.\n\nIn rare cases, a sudden emotional shock is linked to a temporary heart condition called takotsubo cardiomyopathy, nicknamed broken heart syndrome, where part of the heart muscle suddenly weakens. Most people recover. The broader point is that grief is not weakness. It is a whole-body response to a real and painful change in your world."],
    ["Swinging between two worlds", "Healthy grieving often moves between facing the loss and getting on with life, and most people prove resilient.",
      "Picture a widower who spends a morning crying over old photos, then an afternoon learning to cook his wife's meals, then an evening watching football and briefly forgetting. Dutch researchers Margaret Stroebe and Henk Schut described this in their dual process model. One side is loss-oriented: remembering, yearning, feeling the pain. The other is restoration-oriented: handling practical tasks and taking breaks from sorrow. People cope best moving between the two. Laughing at a funeral is not a betrayal.\n\nPsychologist George Bonanno found that the most common pattern after bereavement was not long collapse but real sadness with fairly stable functioning. People can absorb more loss than we assume, and there is no need to force one kind of grief."],
    ["Keeping the bond", "Many mourners stay connected to the person they lost, and that can be healthy rather than a failure to move on.",
      "For much of the twentieth century, the advice was to let go: detach from the dead and reinvest your feelings elsewhere. In 1996 a book called Continuing Bonds, edited by Dennis Klass, Phyllis Silverman and Steven Nickman, challenged that idea. It argued that many mourners keep an ongoing relationship with the person who died, and that this is often comforting.\n\nThink of someone who talks to a late parent at the cemetery, wears a grandmother's ring, cooks a family recipe on an anniversary, or asks what a lost friend would say about a decision. Across many cultures, rituals honour ancestors and keep their memory alive. The goal of grieving is not to forget. It is to find a new place for the person in a life that continues."],
    ["Hidden and prolonged grief", "Some griefs go unacknowledged, and for a minority grief stays intense long enough to need professional support.",
      "Not every loss comes with a funeral or sympathy cards. Researcher Kenneth Doka coined the term disenfranchised grief for losses that are not openly acknowledged, such as a miscarriage, the death of an ex-partner, a pet, or a parent slowly lost to dementia. Naming the loss helps, as does a small ritual like planting a tree. Simply saying 'I know this matters, and I'm sorry' can mean more than advice.\n\nFor most people the sharpest pain softens over months and years. For a minority, intense yearning and an inability to re-engage with life persist; this is now recognised as prolonged grief disorder, and effective therapies exist. This is general information, not medical advice. Talk to your GP, see healthdirect, or call Lifeline on 13 11 14 if you are struggling."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Who were Kübler-Ross's five stages originally based on?", ["People who had lost a family member","Terminally ill patients","Doctors and nurses","Children in hospital"], 1, "The stages came from dying patients and were never meant as a fixed path for mourners."],
    [1, "What does the body's response to grief suggest?", ["Grief is a normal whole-body reaction, not a weakness","Physical symptoms mean something is seriously wrong with the mourner","Concentration problems show the person is not coping","Grief mostly affects the heart in the long term"], 0, "Loss is a major stress, so sleep, appetite and concentration can all be affected."],
    [2, "According to the dual process model, how do people tend to cope best?", ["By staying focused on the loss for as long as possible","By avoiding all reminders of the person","By moving between facing the loss and getting on with life","By feeling grief in a fixed order of stages"], 2, "Moving back and forth between pain and practical life, with respite, helps the mind absorb the loss."],
    [3, "What does the idea of continuing bonds suggest?", ["Mourners should detach from the dead as quickly as possible","Keeping an ongoing connection to the person can be healthy","Grief ends when memories fade","Rituals delay recovery"], 1, "Many people find comfort in staying connected to the person they have lost."],
    [4, "Which is an example of disenfranchised grief?", ["Mourning at a public funeral","Receiving sympathy cards after a death","Taking paid leave after a bereavement","Grieving a miscarriage nobody knew about"], 3, "Losses without social recognition can be harder to carry, so naming them helps."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"The five stages of grief began with dying patients, not mourners."});

WB.L("jealousy-and-envy", "emotions", "intermediate", "Jealousy and envy: the green-eyed emotions",
  "They are often treated as the same ugly feeling, but jealousy and envy are different emotions with different messages. Understand them, and they can tell you surprisingly useful things.",
  [
    ["Two different feelings", "Envy is wanting what someone else has; jealousy is fearing you will lose what you have to a rival.",
      "Your colleague gets the promotion you wanted, and something sour twists inside you. That is envy: a two-person emotion about wanting what another person has, whether their job, looks, house or talent. Now picture your partner laughing a little too warmly with someone at a party. That sinking, protective feeling is jealousy: a three-person emotion about the threat of losing a relationship you value to a rival.\n\nIn everyday speech people use the words interchangeably, saying 'I'm so jealous of your holiday' when they mean envious. Psychologists find the distinction useful because the feelings arise from different situations and push us towards different actions. Envy is about lacking something; jealousy is about guarding something."],
    ["Ancient, universal, useful", "Envy and jealousy are universal and may have evolved to flag threats and gaps that matter.",
      "These emotions are ancient. Envy is among the seven deadly sins of Christian tradition, many cultures warn of the evil eye, and in Shakespeare's Othello, Iago calls jealousy the green-eyed monster while feeding it. Anthropologists find both emotions in societies worldwide, which suggests they are part of human nature. Feeling them is not a moral failure; what you do next matters far more.\n\nEvolutionary psychologists such as David Buss argue that jealousy helped people guard valuable relationships against rivals, while envy may have helped ancestors notice others getting ahead in food, status or allies. These are theories, and scientists debate the details. But your twinge is your mind flagging something it thinks matters. The trouble comes when the alarm is too sensitive, or pushes you towards hostility instead of effort."],
    ["Benign and malicious envy", "One kind of envy motivates you to improve, while the other makes you want to drag others down.",
      "Dutch psychologist Niels van de Ven and colleagues have studied two flavours of envy. Benign envy feels like 'I want that too', and it tends to motivate people to work harder and lift themselves up. Malicious envy feels like 'I wish they didn't have it', and it tends to produce resentment, gossip and a wish to pull the other person down. A close cousin is schadenfreude, a German word for the pleasure people sometimes feel at someone else's misfortune.\n\nResearch suggests which kind arises depends partly on whether we think the other person deserved their success and whether we believe we could achieve something similar. If success seems earned and attainable, benign envy is more likely. That gives you a lever: focus on what is within your reach."],
    ["The comparison machine", "We judge ourselves by comparing with others, and social media supercharges that habit.",
      "In 1954 the American psychologist Leon Festinger proposed social comparison theory: people evaluate their abilities and lives partly by comparing themselves with others. Comparing upward, with people who seem better off, can inspire or deflate. Comparing downward can comfort but also breed smugness.\n\nToday we carry an endless comparison machine in our pockets. Feeds tend to show other people's highlights: the holiday, the renovation, the engagement, the perfect sourdough. You are comparing your behind-the-scenes footage with everyone else's trailer. Studies on social media and wellbeing have produced mixed results, but passive scrolling through curated lives is often linked with more envy than actively chatting with friends. If an account reliably leaves you feeling worse, muting it is not petty. It is sensible maintenance."],
    ["Feelings as information", "Controlling jealousy is a warning sign, while treating envy and jealousy as information can make them useful.",
      "A little jealousy in a relationship can signal that you care and want reassurance, and talking about it honestly often brings couples closer. But checking a partner's phone, tracking their location, restricting who they see or raging over imagined betrayals are forms of control, not romance. In Australia, coercive control is increasingly recognised as a form of family and domestic violence. 1800RESPECT (1800 737 732) offers confidential support.\n\nOtherwise, treat the feelings as data. When envy bites, ask what exactly you want; it may point to a craving for independence or creativity. With jealousy, ask whether the threat is real or a story your mind is telling. Naming the feeling softens it, and gratitude seems to dampen envy. The person you envy may be envying someone else."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is the key difference between envy and jealousy?", ["Envy is wanting what another has; jealousy is fearing loss to a rival","Envy is a two-way feeling between friends; jealousy is a solo feeling","Envy is a passing mood; jealousy is a lasting personality trait","The words mean the same thing and differ only by country"], 0, "Envy is about lacking something, jealousy about guarding something."],
    [1, "What does the evolutionary view suggest about envy and jealousy?", ["They are inventions of modern consumer culture","They are proven to serve no purpose at all","They are moral failures that people should eliminate","They may have evolved as alarms for threats and gaps"], 3, "Feeling them is normal, and the twinge may point to something your mind thinks matters."],
    [2, "When is benign envy most likely to arise?", ["When success seems unearned and out of reach","When success seems earned and attainable","When you dislike the other person","When you cannot see what they have"], 1, "Earned, reachable success tends to motivate effort rather than resentment."],
    [3, "Why does scrolling social media often increase envy?", ["Feeds mostly show others' highlights, inviting unfair comparison","Feeds hide most people's successes and show only failures","Comparison only happens in person, never on a screen","Passive scrolling stops people comparing themselves at all"], 0, "Comparing your everyday life with others' curated highlights tilts the contest."],
    [4, "What is a useful question to ask when you feel envious?", ["How can I make them lose it?","Why am I so bad at everything?","What exactly do I want?","How can I avoid thinking about it?"], 2, "Envy points to something you value, and naming it points towards action."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Envy and jealousy feel alike but are not the same. The difference matters."});

WB.L("forgiveness-science", "emotions", "intermediate", "Forgiveness: what it is, and what it isn't",
  "Forgiving someone does not mean forgetting, excusing or letting them back into your life. Here is what forgiveness actually involves, and why researchers think it can lighten the load.",
  [
    ["Setting the stone down", "Forgiveness means gradually releasing resentment, and it is not excusing, forgetting or reconciling.",
      "Picture someone who, twenty years after a bitter family falling out, still replays the argument in the shower and feels their jaw tighten at the person's name. The hurt took five minutes; the resentment has taken two decades. Psychologists call this replaying rumination, and it keeps anger and distress fresh. Forgiveness, in the research sense, is about gradually setting that stone down.\n\nIt does not mean saying what happened was acceptable, forgetting it, or trusting the person again. Reconciliation, restoring a relationship, takes two people and requires the other to be safe and trustworthy. Forgiveness can happen entirely inside one person, even if the other never apologises or has died. You can forgive and still keep firm boundaries, report a crime or end a relationship."],
    ["Evidence and REACH", "People who forgive more readily report less stress and more wellbeing, though cause and effect are hard to separate.",
      "People who forgive more readily generally report less anxiety, less depression and greater life satisfaction. In experiments, people imagining a grudge showed bigger rises in heart rate and blood pressure than when imagining forgiving. Structured programmes by Robert Enright and Everett Worthington have shown benefits in trials. But much of the evidence is correlational: forgiving may make people healthier, or calmer people may forgive more easily. Probably both.\n\nWorthington's REACH approach has five steps. Recall the hurt honestly. Empathise, seeing the other person as a whole human without excusing them. Give the altruistic gift of forgiveness, remembering times you were forgiven. Commit, perhaps by writing a letter you never send. Hold on, because old feelings resurface and that does not mean it failed."],
    ["Forgiveness on a national scale", "Societies have used truth-telling and public acknowledgement to heal, which shows forgiveness is social as well as personal.",
      "After apartheid ended, South Africa faced an enormous question: how could people who had suffered terrible abuses live alongside those responsible? One answer was the Truth and Reconciliation Commission, chaired by Archbishop Desmond Tutu in the late 1990s. Victims could tell their stories publicly, and perpetrators could apply for amnesty if they fully disclosed politically motivated crimes. Tutu championed the view that there is no future without forgiveness, the title of his book about the process.\n\nThe commission was praised worldwide and also criticised, with some victims feeling justice was sacrificed. In Australia, the 2008 National Apology to the Stolen Generations showed how public acknowledgement of harm can matter deeply."],
    ["Forgiving yourself", "Genuine self-forgiveness includes taking responsibility, not just letting yourself off the hook.",
      "Sometimes the person we cannot forgive is ourselves. Maybe you said something cruel to a parent before they died, or a mistake at work hurt others. Researchers distinguish genuine self-forgiveness from simply excusing yourself. Pseudo self-forgiveness skips responsibility: 'It wasn't really my fault.'\n\nGenuine self-forgiveness starts by owning what you did, feeling appropriate remorse and making amends where possible, then releasing ongoing self-punishment. This links to self-compassion, popularised by psychologist Kristin Neff: treating yourself with the kindness you would offer a friend who had made the same mistake. Endless self-criticism rarely makes people better. It more often makes them defensive, stuck or avoidant. Accepting that you are a flawed human who can learn and repair is usually a better route to doing better."],
    ["Taking your time", "Forgiveness cannot be forced, and it should never be demanded of people who are still unsafe.",
      "Forgiveness is not a switch you flick. For serious harms it can take years, and some people decide not to forgive certain things, which is their right. Pressuring someone to forgive, especially a person who has been abused, can add to their pain and even keep them in danger. Safety and justice come first. If you are experiencing abuse, 1800RESPECT (1800 737 732) offers confidential support, and in an emergency call 000.\n\nFor everyday hurts, small steps help: noticing when you are rehearsing the grievance, wondering what was going on for the other person, and choosing to stop feeding the story. This is general information, not therapy. If an old hurt still dominates your life, a psychologist, counsellor or your GP can help. Forgiving is ultimately something you do for your own freedom."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Which statement about forgiveness is true according to researchers?", ["It requires you to trust the person again","It means that the hurt was acceptable","It can happen without an apology","It is the same thing as reconciliation"], 2, "Forgiveness can happen entirely inside one person, separate from excusing or reconciling."],
    [1, "Why is it hard to say forgiveness causes better health?", ["Forgiveness has never been studied","Much of the evidence is correlational","Forgiving people report more stress","Trials have found no benefits"], 1, "Calmer, healthier people may simply find forgiving easier, so the link may run both ways."],
    [2, "What did South Africa's Truth and Reconciliation Commission offer perpetrators?", ["Automatic pardon for any crime, with no conditions","Free legal defence if they were taken to court","Amnesty if they fully disclosed political crimes","Reduced prison time for apologising privately"], 2, "Public truth-telling was central, though some victims felt justice was sacrificed."],
    [3, "What distinguishes genuine self-forgiveness from pseudo self-forgiveness?", ["Owning what you did and making amends","Never feeling any remorse about it","Blaming the circumstances you were in","Trying to forget the mistake entirely"], 0, "Genuine self-forgiveness takes responsibility and then releases ongoing self-punishment."],
    [4, "Why should forgiveness never be demanded of someone who is unsafe?", ["Forgiving always ends a relationship","Pressure can add to their pain and danger","Forgiveness is only possible after reconciling","Safety is unrelated to forgiveness"], 1, "Safety and justice come first, and forgiveness cannot be forced."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Forgiveness can happen entirely inside one person. Here is what that means."});

WB.L("shame-and-guilt", "emotions", "advanced", "Shame and guilt: the self-conscious emotions",
  "They both make your face burn, but shame and guilt pull you in opposite directions. One tends to help you repair; the other tends to make you hide. Learning the difference can change how you treat yourself and others.",
  [
    ["Two kinds of sting", "Guilt focuses on what you did, while shame focuses on who you are.",
      "Imagine you forget a close friend's birthday. One inner voice says, 'I did a thoughtless thing, I should call and make it up to her.' Another says, 'I'm a terrible friend, I always let people down.' Both are painful, but they are different emotions. Psychologist June Tangney and her colleagues, who have studied this for decades, put it neatly: guilt is about a bad thing I did, while shame is about being a bad self.\n\nThe behaviour is the same, but the focus differs. Guilt targets a specific action that can be changed. Shame targets the whole person, which feels far harder to fix. That small shift in focus has big consequences for what people do next."],
    ["Self-conscious emotions", "Shame and guilt are self-conscious emotions shaped by both biology and culture.",
      "Fear and joy appear early, but shame, guilt, embarrassment and pride emerge later, typically in the toddler years, once children see themselves as separate people who can be evaluated. These self-conscious emotions involve imagining how you look to others. They likely helped our ancestors live in groups, nudging people to follow shared rules, repair damage and avoid rejection. Darwin was fascinated by blushing, which he saw as a uniquely human expression.\n\nIn 1946 anthropologist Ruth Benedict contrasted 'shame cultures' with 'guilt cultures'. Later scholars found this too neat, and she wrote without visiting Japan. People everywhere feel both emotions, though cultures differ in triggers and expression. No society runs on only one."],
    ["Repair or hide", "Guilt tends to motivate repair, while shame tends to provoke hiding or lashing out.",
      "People prone to guilt rather than shame are more likely to apologise, accept responsibility and put things right. Guilt says the problem is the action, so the solution is a better action. Tangney's research links guilt-proneness with empathy. Excessive guilt over things that were not your fault is exhausting, but healthy guilt works like a moral compass.\n\nShame says the problem is you, and since you cannot swap yourself out, the instinct is to escape. People avoid eye contact, stop answering messages or hide the mistake. Shame-proneness is also linked with defensiveness and anger: 'It's not my fault, it's yours.' That is why shaming people often backfires. Brené Brown argues that shame thrives on secrecy and silence, and loses some grip when spoken about."],
    ["Online pile-ons", "Online shaming is fast and often out of proportion, and it can harden people instead of prompting reflection.",
      "Public shaming is ancient. Communities once used stocks, pillories and public notices to humiliate wrongdoers. Social media has revived the practice at enormous scale. A clumsy joke or an angry moment caught on camera can be shared millions of times within hours, and the target may face abuse, lose a job and find their name permanently linked to the worst moment of their life. Writer Jon Ronson explored such cases in his 2015 book So You've Been Publicly Shamed.\n\nAccountability for genuine harm matters, but crowds rarely calibrate the punishment to the offence, and the shame-anger cycle can make people dig in rather than reflect. Before joining a pile-on, ask whether you are aiming for repair, or just enjoying the feeling of righteousness."],
    ["Turning shame around", "Separating your actions from your worth, and treating yourself with self-compassion, shifts shame towards healthy guilt.",
      "The split between shame and guilt is partly about how we talk to ourselves, and that can be practised. When you catch yourself thinking 'I'm an idiot', try 'I made a mistake, and here's what I can do about it.' That moves the focus from your whole self to a specific action. Research on self-compassion suggests that responding to failure with kindness, rather than harsh self-attack, makes people more likely to take responsibility and improve, not less.\n\nTalking to a trusted person also helps, because shame shrinks when shared and met with understanding. This is general information, not therapy. If shame feels constant or overwhelming, a GP or psychologist can help, healthdirect lists mental health services across Australia, and Lifeline (13 11 14) is there any time if you are struggling to cope or feel unsafe."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "According to June Tangney's framing, what is the core difference between guilt and shame?", ["Guilt is private, while shame is public","Guilt is about the act; shame is about the self","Guilt lasts longer than shame does","Guilt is a thought, shame is a behaviour"], 1, "Guilt targets a changeable action, while shame targets the whole person."],
    [1, "What is wrong with a simple split into shame cultures and guilt cultures?", ["Cultures never differ in how emotions are expressed","The split was proven by decades of fieldwork","People everywhere feel both emotions to some degree","Shame and guilt do not exist in some cultures"], 2, "Culture shapes emotions, but the neat two-way split is considered too simple."],
    [2, "What is a common response to shame, according to research?", ["Apologising and making amends","Withdrawing, hiding or blaming others","Calmly accepting responsibility","Seeking out a sympathetic audience"], 1, "Because shame says the problem is you, people tend to escape or protect themselves by turning outward."],
    [3, "Why does public shaming often fail to produce change?", ["It is rarely noticed by the person targeted","It usually fades within a few minutes","It always leads straight to repair and reflection","It is out of proportion and triggers defensiveness"], 3, "Pile-ons can cause hiding, denial or hostility rather than reflection."],
    [4, "Which self-talk is more likely to shift shame towards healthy guilt?", ["'I made a mistake, and here is what I can do.'","'I'm an idiot, and I always get this wrong.'","'It's someone else's fault, not mine.'","'I always let people down, so why try?'"], 0, "Focusing on a specific action keeps the problem fixable and the person intact."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Guilt and shame sound alike, yet they push you in opposite directions."});

WB.L("nostalgia-science", "emotions", "beginner", "Nostalgia: why we long for the good old days",
  "Once considered a dangerous disease, nostalgia turns out to be one of the mind's quiet comforts. Here is why an old song can make you feel warmer, more connected and more like yourself.",
  [
    ["A disease of homesickness", "Nostalgia was named in 1688 as a medical condition, from Greek words for homecoming and pain.",
      "In 1688 a Swiss medical student, Johannes Hofer, wrote about a strange illness affecting young people far from home, including Swiss soldiers serving abroad. Sufferers were listless, lost their appetite and pined obsessively for their native mountains. Hofer coined a word for it by combining the Greek nostos, meaning homecoming, with algos, meaning pain. Nostalgia literally meant the pain of longing to return home.\n\nFor a long time doctors treated it as a serious condition, and some blamed everything from changes in air pressure to the clanging of cowbells. Over the following centuries the meaning shifted from homesickness for a place to a sentimental longing for the past. In recent decades, researchers have found that nostalgia is usually far more helpful than harmful."],
    ["Bittersweet, mostly sweet", "Nostalgia is mostly positive, and it boosts connection, meaning and the sense of being the same person over time.",
      "A song from your first summer job or the smell of your family's beach sunscreen brings warmth tinged with a faint ache. Research led by Constantine Sedikides and Tim Wildschut at the University of Southampton suggests nostalgia is predominantly positive. Nostalgic stories often follow a redemption pattern, starting with something hard or ordinary and ending on a positive note, and they tend to feature loved ones.\n\nIn experiments, recalling a nostalgic memory, compared with an ordinary one, increased feelings of being loved, raised self-esteem, strengthened a sense of meaning and boosted optimism. It also builds self-continuity: the sense that today's you is connected to the you of years ago. In times of change, such as moving, retiring or becoming a parent, that thread is steadying."],
    ["A mood repair kit", "People often turn to nostalgia when they feel lonely, bored or unsettled, and it helps restore connection.",
      "Researchers have found that negative feelings, especially loneliness, tend to trigger nostalgia. When people feel isolated, their minds drift to memories of being close to others, which seems to restore a sense of connection. Boredom and uncertainty can also prompt nostalgic reflection, perhaps as a way of reaching for meaning.\n\nSome studies even link nostalgia with physical warmth. In one set of experiments, people felt more nostalgic on colder days, and recalling nostalgic memories seemed to make them feel warmer and more tolerant of cold. The findings are intriguing rather than definitive, but they fit a pattern. Nostalgia works like an internal comfort blanket, something the mind reaches for when the present feels a bit chilly."],
    ["Why youth looms large", "We remember events from our teens to our late twenties more vividly because they were formative and full of firsts.",
      "Ask older adults for important memories or favourite music, and a pattern emerges. Memories cluster between about age ten and thirty, a phenomenon psychologists call the reminiscence bump. That period is packed with firsts: first love, first job, first time living away from home. It is also when people form their adult identity, so those events become part of their life story. The brain may encode novel, emotional experiences especially well.\n\nThis is why the songs of your youth hit differently, and why so many people believe music was better when they were young. It probably was not better. It was simply woven into the most formative chapter of your life."],
    ["Using nostalgia well", "Nostalgia sells and can mislead, but it works best as comfort and direction rather than a place to live.",
      "Marketers, film studios and politicians know nostalgia is persuasive. Retro packaging, rebooted franchises and 1980s songs in advertisements are no accident. Nostalgia makes people feel safe and warm, and some research suggests it can make them more willing to spend. The good old days are also remembered selectively, with hardships trimmed away. A nostalgic glow is lovely, but it is not a reliable history book.\n\nSome people get stuck comparing a disappointing present with an idealised past, a habit called rosy retrospection. The trick is to use nostalgia as fuel rather than a refuge: make a playlist from your school years, cook a grandparent's recipe, or message an old friend to share a memory. Notice, too, tomorrow's nostalgia in today's ordinary moments."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did the word nostalgia originally describe?", ["A love of old music","A fear of travel","A sentimental longing for the past","The pain of longing to return home"], 3, "Hofer joined Greek roots for homecoming and pain, naming homesickness as a medical condition."],
    [1, "What does research suggest nostalgia does for people?", ["Increases connection, meaning and optimism","Leaves people feeling lonelier and more cut off","Weakens people's sense of who they are","Lowers self-esteem and general optimism"], 0, "Nostalgic memories tend to feature loved ones and leave people feeling supported."],
    [2, "Which feeling especially tends to trigger nostalgia?", ["Pride","Anger","Loneliness","Excitement"], 2, "The mind reaches for memories of closeness when people feel isolated or unsettled."],
    [3, "What is the reminiscence bump?", ["Memories cluster most between about ages ten and thirty","Memories fade fastest during middle age and later life","Songs from our youth are objectively the best ever made","The most recent memories are always the most vivid"], 0, "Youth is full of firsts and identity formation, so those years loom large in memory."],
    [4, "What is rosy retrospection?", ["Remembering mostly your past failures","Enjoying nostalgic advertising too much","Idealising the past and finding the present dull","Recreating old family rituals each year"], 2, "Nostalgia helps most when used as fuel and not as a refuge from the present."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Nostalgia was once diagnosed as a medical illness. Now it looks like a comfort."});

WB.L("science-of-awe", "emotions", "beginner", "Awe: the emotion that makes you feel small, in a good way",
  "Standing under a star-filled sky, hearing a choir swell, watching a stranger's act of courage. Awe is one of the least studied emotions, yet it may be one of the most powerful for our wellbeing.",
  [
    ["Under a desert sky", "Awe is the feeling of encountering something vast that stretches your understanding.",
      "Picture lying on red earth in outback Australia, far from any town, as the Milky Way spills across the sky in a band so bright it casts a faint glow. You might feel tiny, a little dizzy and strangely calm. That is awe.\n\nFor a long time psychologists paid it little attention, focusing on fear and anger. That changed in 2003, when Dacher Keltner and Jonathan Haidt published an influential paper defining awe as a response to vastness, something much larger than the self, combined with a need for accommodation, meaning your mental frameworks must stretch to make sense of it. It can come from nature, art, music, ideas or people. Awe is the emotion of wonder at the edges of understanding."],
    ["Small self, kinder self", "Awe shrinks self-importance, which feels freeing and can make people more generous and helpful.",
      "In awe research, one consistent finding is the small self. People often feel smaller, and in experiments sometimes draw themselves smaller. Most find it relieving: everyday worries seem to shrink alongside the self. Early brain imaging suggests awe may quieten self-focused thinking.\n\nIn one study, Paul Piff's team had participants gaze for a minute at towering eucalyptus trees on a California campus, while others looked at a plain building. Afterwards, someone nearby dropped a handful of pens. Those who had looked at the trees picked up more. Other experiments found that awe, induced through videos of natural wonders, made people more generous in sharing resources. When your own concerns feel less pressing, other people's needs come into sharper focus."],
    ["Your body on awe", "Awe is a whole-body experience, with goosebumps, chills and often a calmer body.",
      "Have you felt shivers down your spine during powerful music, or the hair on your arms rise at the top of a mountain? Goosebumps and chills are commonly reported during awe. Some researchers suggest awe is linked with the vagus nerve, part of the system that helps calm the body, and people often report slower breathing, widened eyes and a dropped jaw, the classic expression of amazement.\n\nOne line of research has linked frequent positive emotions, awe among them, with lower levels of inflammatory markers, though such findings are early and should not be overstated. What seems clear is that awe is not just a thought. It is a whole-body experience, which makes it noticeably different from simple happiness or excitement."],
    ["From orbit to other people", "Astronauts describe the overview effect, but everyday sources of awe, especially other people, work too.",
      "Looking back at Earth from orbit, many astronauts see a fragile blue ball in black space with no borders between nations. In 1987 writer Frank White coined the term overview effect for this shift: a sense of the unity of life, concern for the planet and impatience with conflicts that look trivial from that height. The Earthrise photograph from Apollo 8 in 1968 gave many people on the ground a taste of it.\n\nYou do not need a rocket. Keltner's research, gathering stories from people in many countries, found that one of the most common sources of awe is other people, especially courage, kindness or overcoming adversity, which he calls moral beauty. Others include nature, singing together, music, art, big ideas, and holding a newborn."],
    ["Awe walks", "A weekly awe walk can raise positive emotions and lower daily distress.",
      "In a study published in 2020, researchers asked older adults to take a weekly fifteen-minute walk. Some were simply told to walk, while others were encouraged to take an awe walk, deliberately looking for things that amazed them, from the intricate pattern of a leaf to the sweep of clouds.\n\nOver eight weeks, the awe walkers reported more positive emotions and less daily distress. In photos they took of themselves, they also made themselves smaller in the frame and smiled more. You can try a version of this anywhere. Look up at the night sky, watch the sunrise, listen to a piece of music with full attention, or read about the scale of the universe. Awe seems to be a free, renewable resource that most of us underuse."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "According to Keltner and Haidt, awe involves vastness and what else?", ["A strong fear of what lies ahead","A need to stretch your thinking","Complete calm with no surprise at all","A wish to get away from it"], 1, "Awe combines something much larger than the self with a need to accommodate it in your thinking."],
    [1, "In Paul Piff's tree study, what did people who looked at tall eucalyptus trees do?", ["They picked up more of the dropped pens","They left the study earlier than others","They ignored the person who dropped pens","They took more photos of the trees"], 0, "Awe shrinks self-focus, so other people's needs come into sharper focus."],
    [2, "Which body response is commonly reported during awe?", ["Goosebumps or shivers down the spine","A clenched jaw and tense shoulders","Heavy drowsiness and constant yawning","Complete numbness in the limbs"], 0, "Goosebumps and chills are commonly reported, which is why awe is called a whole-body experience."],
    [3, "What is the overview effect?", ["A new perspective felt on seeing Earth from space","A fear of heights felt on tall buildings","A loss of interest in the natural world","The belief that Earth is small and unimportant"], 0, "Seeing Earth as a borderless, fragile ball can foster unity and concern for the planet."],
    [4, "What did participants on awe walks report over eight weeks?", ["More daily distress and fewer smiles","More positive emotions and less distress","No difference from ordinary walks","Greater tiredness and sore joints"], 1, "Deliberately looking for things that amaze you brings more awe into daily life."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A one-minute look at tall trees made people measurably more helpful."});

WB.L("art-of-apology", "emotions", "beginner", "How to say sorry: the art of a good apology",
  "Few words carry as much power as 'I'm sorry', and few are so often fumbled. Here is what research and real life reveal about apologies that actually heal, and the ones that make things worse.",
  [
    ["Why apologies matter", "A sincere apology can calm anger, rebuild trust and make forgiveness more likely.",
      "Think of a time someone genuinely apologised to you. Often something softens at once: the anger loosens and the story you were telling yourself shifts.\n\nA good apology does real social work. It admits a norm was broken, shows you understand the harm, and signals that you value the relationship enough to swallow some pride. Research has found a sincere apology can reduce anger and make forgiveness more likely, while a poor one, or none, can deepen the wound.\n\nYet many of us find apologising hard, because admitting fault can feel like losing status or inviting blame. Knowing what makes an apology work makes it easier to do well, and to do sooner."],
    ["The key ingredients", "The most effective apologies own the mistake specifically and offer to fix it.",
      "Counsellors and researchers broadly agree on the core parts of a strong apology.\n\nFirst, name what you did, specifically: \"I interrupted you repeatedly in that meeting\", not a vague \"Sorry about earlier.\" Second, take responsibility without shifting blame. Third, express genuine regret and show you understand how it affected the other person. Fourth, offer to repair the damage where you can, and commit to doing better.\n\nA brief explanation can help, but it needs care, because explanations easily slide into excuses. A study led by Roy Lewicki in the United States found that acknowledging responsibility was the most important ingredient, followed closely by an offer of repair. People care most about you owning it and fixing it."],
    ["The non-apology", "Apologies that dodge responsibility, such as \"sorry if you were offended\", often make people angrier.",
      "\"I'm sorry if anyone was offended.\" \"Mistakes were made.\" \"I'm sorry you feel that way.\" These are classic non-apologies: they use the word sorry while dodging responsibility.\n\nThe word \"if\" implies the harm may not be real. The passive \"mistakes were made\" hides who made them. \"I'm sorry you feel that way\" puts the problem in the other person's feelings, not your actions. Another trap is the \"sorry, but\" apology, where everything after the \"but\" undoes what came before.\n\nPeople see through these quickly, and research suggests a poor apology can be worse than saying nothing, because it adds insult to injury. If you catch yourself reaching for \"if\" or \"but\", pause and ask what you actually did."],
    ["Sorry in public life", "Open apologies can carry great weight in public life, and Australian law generally protects a simple \"sorry\" from counting as an admission of fault.",
      "Apologies matter on a national scale too. On 13 February 2008, Prime Minister Kevin Rudd delivered the National Apology to Australia's Indigenous peoples in federal Parliament, particularly the Stolen Generations: Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children forcibly removed from their families under government policies. He said sorry three times. Crowds watched on big screens across the country, and many wept. The 1997 Bringing Them Home report had recommended an apology, and the words weighed heavily because they openly acknowledged real harm after years of refusal.\n\nOn the legal side, every Australian state and territory has laws that generally stop a simple apology being treated as an admission of fault in many civil cases. Details vary, and insurers may ask you not to admit fault, so check your policy or ask a lawyer."],
    ["Saying and hearing sorry", "Save sorry for real harm, swap needless ones for thanks, and receive apologies honestly.",
      "Some people say sorry constantly: when bumped into, before asking a question, when sending a reasonable email. Over-apologising can make you seem less confident, water down a genuine apology and shift blame onto you unfairly. Try swapping some sorrys for thank yous: \"Thanks for waiting\" instead of \"Sorry I'm late\". It recognises the other person's kindness rather than your fault.\n\nReceiving an apology is a skill too. Waving it away with \"It's fine\" when it wasn't can leave the hurt unacknowledged. Try instead: \"Thank you for saying that. It did upset me.\" You needn't forgive on the spot, but once someone has made amends, repeatedly raising it stops healing. Well-received apologies often leave relationships stronger."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What can a sincere apology do?", ["Reduce anger and make forgiveness more likely","Settle who was right in the dispute","Remove any need to change your behaviour","Work best when delayed for several weeks"], 0, "A sincere apology acknowledges harm, which softens anger and opens the way to forgiveness."],
    [1, "According to Lewicki's study, which ingredient matters most in an apology?", ["A detailed explanation of your reasons","A request for immediate forgiveness","Visible emotion such as tears or distress","Owning the mistake and offering to fix it"], 3, "People care most that you own what you did and offer to repair it."],
    [2, "Why is \"I'm sorry if anyone was offended\" a non-apology?", ["It is too brief to come across as sincere","It casts doubt on the harm and avoids responsibility","It names the person who was hurt directly","It offers to repair the damage that was done"], 1, "The word \"if\" casts doubt on the harm and dodges ownership of the action."],
    [3, "What does Australian law generally do about simple apologies?", ["It treats them as proof of fault in most court cases","It requires them to be written down and witnessed","It generally stops them counting as an admission of fault","It bans them after accidents unless a lawyer is present"], 2, "Apology laws exist to encourage human decency, though details vary, so seek advice if worried."],
    [4, "What is a helpful swap for someone who over-apologises?", ["Stay silent in meetings","Replace some sorrys with thank yous","Apologise twice as often as before","Use more formal wording in emails"], 1, "Thanking someone recognises their kindness instead of focusing on your supposed fault."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Some apologies make things worse. Here is what the good ones have in common."});

WB.L("siblings-science", "emotions", "beginner", "Siblings: the longest relationship of your life",
  "Your brothers and sisters often share more years of your life than your parents, partner or children do. Here is what research says about rivalry, birth order myths, only children and why sibling bonds matter so much.",
  [
    ["The longest relationship", "Siblings can share more of your life than parents, partners or friends, so the bond matters.",
      "Who will be around for your whole life? Parents usually die before us, and partners and friends tend to arrive later. Siblings can share almost every chapter, from childhood bath times to retirement, often across seven or eight decades.\n\nSiblings share parents, homes, family jokes and memories nobody else holds. They can be best friends, fierce rivals, or both on the same day.\n\nResearchers have long focused on parents and partners, but interest in siblings is growing. These relationships shape how we learn to share, fight, negotiate and love, and they often matter more in old age than people expect."],
    ["Rivalry and training", "Rivalry mostly comes from competition and perceived unfairness, yet siblings also give constant practice in cooperation and empathy.",
      "Sibling conflict is extremely common in childhood; studies of young siblings at home have recorded squabbles many times a day, often over parents' time and approval.\n\nLasting resentment seems to come less from conflict itself than from differential treatment: children believing a parent favours one of them. What matters most is often whether the difference seems fair. Extra homework help for a younger child may be accepted, while unexplained favouritism lingers. Parents who explain their reasons and avoid comparisons can ease rivalry.\n\nFor all the fighting, siblings give daily practice at sharing, standing up for yourself and making up. Warm sibling bonds are linked with better emotional understanding, and a supportive sibling can buffer family stress. Ongoing bullying by a sibling is harmful, though, and shouldn't be dismissed as normal rivalry."],
    ["The birth order myth", "Large studies find little evidence that birth order shapes personality the way popular culture claims.",
      "Popular wisdom says firstborns are bossy achievers, middle children are overlooked peacemakers, and youngest children are charming, rebellious and spoiled. It is a satisfying story, and most of us can think of examples.\n\nBut in large samples the effects mostly vanish. A 2015 study by German psychologists Julia Rohrer, Boris Egloff and Stefan Schmukle used data from over twenty thousand people in Germany, the United States and the United Kingdom. It found no meaningful birth order effects on traits such as extraversion, agreeableness or conscientiousness. It did find a very small average advantage for firstborns on intelligence measures, far too small to matter for any individual.\n\nThe stereotypes persist because they make tidy stories, and we remember the examples that fit."],
    ["Why siblings differ", "Siblings differ because each child experiences the family differently, through experiences they don't share.",
      "Many parents marvel at how different their children are, despite the same home and the same parents. Siblings share, on average, about half of their genetic variation, so there is plenty of room for difference.\n\nResearch also points to the non-shared environment: experiences that differ between siblings, such as different friends, teachers, hobbies, illnesses or even different treatment by parents. Scientists find that much of the environmental influence on personality comes from these, not from the family home as a whole.\n\nA family is not one environment but several. Each child arrives at a different time with a different temperament, and parents change over the years. Siblings may also carve out different niches, one the sporty one and another the bookish one, to stand out."],
    ["Only children and adult siblings", "Only children turn out much like others, and sibling bonds often warm again later in life.",
      "Only children have long had a bad reputation. In the late nineteenth century the American psychologist G. Stanley Hall reportedly called being an only child a disease in itself. Later research, including reviews by Toni Falbo, has generally found only children are much like others on personality, sociability and adjustment. Differences are small, and some favour only children, such as slightly higher achievement. They gain social skills through cousins, friends and school.\n\nIn adulthood, sibling contact often drops as careers and families take over, yet many bonds warm again in midlife and old age. Caring for ageing parents can bring siblings together, or reignite tensions over fairness and money. Some relationships do break down. If you have drifted from a sibling, a small step, like sharing an old photo, can reopen the door."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why are siblings often described as the longest relationship of a person's life?", ["They can share most of life, from childhood to old age","They almost always live near each other as adults","The law requires them to stay in regular contact","They are born with a stronger bond than other kin"], 0, "Siblings can be present across seven or eight decades, longer than parents, partners or friends."],
    [1, "What is a major driver of lasting sibling resentment?", ["Sharing a bedroom as children","Having different hobbies","Feeling a parent plays favourites","A gap of several years in age"], 2, "Perceived unfair favouritism, more than conflict itself, tends to leave lasting resentment."],
    [2, "What did the 2015 study of over twenty thousand people find about birth order and personality?", ["Firstborns are much more extraverted","Youngest children are far more rebellious","Middle children are noticeably less agreeable","No meaningful effects on personality traits"], 3, "Birth order stereotypes fade in large samples because we mainly remember examples that fit them."],
    [3, "What is the non-shared environment?", ["Experiences that differ between siblings","A home where siblings live in separate houses","The genes that siblings do not have in common","The part of a child's life spent only at school"], 0, "Different friends, teachers, illnesses and treatment make one family into several environments."],
    [4, "What has research generally found about only children?", ["They are much more selfish than others","They struggle to develop social skills","They are much like children with siblings","They are nearly always lonely"], 2, "The stereotype of the lonely, spoiled only child isn't well supported by research."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Birth order shapes personality, or so we all believe. Does the evidence agree?"});

